uld not indict a sitting  president but such a policy tribe argues  would be unconstitutional and tribe  actually points out  specifically that the strategy predicted  by conservative columnist Jennifer Rubin  earlier this week is likely to be  exactly why indictment of a sitting  president as possible Rubin suggested  and we did this clip for you earlier in  the week that Donald Trump would resign  ten minutes before the next Democratic  president would be inaugurated that  could be in 2021 that could be in 2025  so that Mike Pence would be President  for those last 10 minutes of Trump's  term and would issue a pre-emptive  pardon making it so that Donald Trump  could not be indicted after he leaves  the White House we know that the framers  of the Constitution took very seriously  that we shouldn't have a king in the  United States and that there have to be  ways to deal with a potentially corrupt  or criminal person becoming president of  the United States so thus leaving  presidents free to sort of cash in these  get-out-of-jail-free cards with  pre-emptive resignation and pardoning by  their vice presidents who could be just  as corrupt as they are we don't know yet  when it comes to Mike Pence but the Vice  President could be just as corrupt as  the president it seems unimaginable as  what the framers would want that they  would want such a system where a  president can effectively make  themselves not vulnerable to any  consequences to their actions the point  that tribe makes in the op-ed which I  encourage you to read yourself is that  no matter what your view is on why you  can't indict a sitting president that it  would be unconstitutional if the Justice  Department has a policy that says we  don't indict sitting presidents tribe  argues that would be unconstitutional it  definitely doesn't seem that such a  formal policy exists but what if the  president can always preemptively have  themselves pardoned by the VP that also  would be unconstitutional if i